498		\uses{def:consistency, def:bracket, def:povm-conventions, lem:ld-sandwich-measurement, fact:agreement, fact:add-a-proj, fact:triangle, fact:data-processing}
499		The source proof is a citation: the argument is identical to the proof of Fact 4.34 in \cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}, with the only modification being the insertion of the dependence on the question $x$ for all measurements considered. The argument has three steps. First, the evaluated consistency \eqref{eq:ld-sandwich-1} is lifted to the polynomial outcomes: if Alice measures $A^x$ and Bob measures $G^{i,\,x}$, the probability that the $i$-th outcomes $g_i, g_i'$ differ is at most the probability that they differ after evaluation at $y$ plus the probability that two distinct functions in $\mathcal G_i$ agree at $y$, so $A^x_{[g_i]} \ot \Id_B \simeq_{\delta+\eps} \Id_A \ot G^{i,\,x}_{g_i}$ on average over $x \sim \mu$. Second, these consistencies are converted into operator distances (Lemma~\ref{fact:agreement}) and the projective factors $G^{i,\,x}_{g_i}$ are inserted next to $A^x_{g_1,\dots,g_k}$ one index at a time (Lemma~\ref{fact:add-a-proj}), each insertion costing $O((\delta+\eps)^{1/2})$ by the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality and the triangle inequality (Lemma~\ref{fact:triangle}); this produces the sandwich $C^x$ of Lemma~\ref{lem:ld-sandwich-measurement} at distance $O(k(\delta+\eps)^{1/2})$ from $A^x$ on the polynomial-tuple outcomes. Third, data processing along the evaluation map $\operatorname{eval}_y$ (Lemma~\ref{fact:data-processing}) returns to the evaluated outcomes $(a_1,\dots,a_k)$ with the same error.
500	\end{proof}
